 "id": "gripper-jaw-fatigue-mjf",
 "brief": "One jaw of my robot gripper, printed in PA12 by MJF. It is driven directly by the gripper servo's 25\nmm horn: the horn face is at z = 0, axis Z through the origin, and the jaw is screwed to it with\nfour M2.5 screws through 2.9 mm holes on a 16 mm circle (at (8,0), (-8,0), (0,8), (0,-8)), heads on\ntop of a 4 mm thick hub disc (z = 0 to 4, 25 mm diameter) that I want solid apart from the holes;\nleave a 6 mm column clear above each screw head (z = 4 up) and a 6 mm hole on the axis for the horn\nscrew. The jaw reaches out along +X to a fingertip pad x = 55 to 75, y = -6 to -2, z = 0 to 12,\nwhose y = -6 face grips. A 4 mm wire for the fingertip sensor runs along the jaw at y = 4, z = 6\nfrom x = 12 to x = 78 (keep it clear). The jaw fits in x = -14 to 78, y = -6 to 14, z = 0 to 12,\nplus anywhere within 14 mm of the horn axis. Every grasp pushes the pad back with 15 N (+Y): a\nmillion grasps over its life, fatigue safety factor 2. Opening, it can jam against the housing with\nthe servo's full 1.8 N·m stall torque (the pad pushed toward -Y, taken at the pad's centre 65 mm\nout), and it gets knocked: 20 N on the pad from any direction. Static safety factor 2, under 9 g.",
